  "query_preview": "..\n(Sunday night through next Saturday)\nIssued at 134 PM CDT Sat Jun 27 2026\n\nAn expansive and strong upper level high will become established \nover much of the the eastern U.S. (centered over the Ohio Valley \nMonday and Tuesday, the central Appalachians Wednesday/Thursday, and \nTennessee/North Carolina Friday). Western CONUS troughing will be \nmaintained to our west. With an embedded disturbance or two forecast \nto lift north-northeast along the New Mexico/Texas border, areas to \nour west (West Texas and adjacent eastern New Mexico) will have \nincreased chances of showers and thunderstorms from mid-afternoon \nthrough late evening Monday and Tuesday. Far western portions of our \narea (far western parts of Fisher and Sterling Counties and \nnorthwestern Crockett County) will have just a slight chance (around \n20 percent) during that time frame.  \n\nSurface pressure gradient is forecast to weaken slightly for the \nlatter half of next week, so by that time it will not be as breezy \nin our area. Seasonably hot temperatures will continue with little \nday-to-day change through Thursday. Could have an uptick in \ntemperatures Friday and Saturday, when a slight strengthening in \nthe 850mb thermal ridge is progged. Looking ahead to the Fourth of\nJuly holiday weekend, seeing some indications for the possibility\nof a weakness to develop in the height field aloft over Texas. \nThis may allow for some possibility of a few diurnally driven \nshowers and thunderstorms. Model blends at this time have PoPs \nless than 15 percent, but it is something we will monitor.",
